meta {
  name: ACME Challenge Responder (Public)
  type: http
  seq: 18
}

get {
  url: {{host}}/.well-known/acme-challenge/not-a-real-token
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("an unknown token is not found", function () {
    // 404 rather than 200-with-empty-body. A CA treats any non-matching
    // response as a failed validation either way, but an empty 200 would make a
    // genuinely broken lookup indistinguishable from a token that expired.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(404);
  });

  test("does not say whether the token was absent or merely expired", function () {
    // A generic message, deliberately. Absent and expired answer identically —
    // the distinction is only useful to someone probing for live tokens, and
    // the CA treats either as a failed validation regardless.
    expect(res.getBody().message).to.equal("Not Found");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /.well-known/acme-challenge/:token

  Answers ACME `http-01` validation requests, so the platform can obtain its own
  TLS certificates — for the platform host, agency subdomains, and agency-owned
  custom domains.

  **Module** — none. **Permission** — none, `@Public()` and `@SkipThrottle()`.

  ## Not under `{{baseUrl}}`

  Note the URL uses `{{host}}`, not `{{baseUrl}}`. RFC 8555 fixes this path at
  the **root** of the host being validated, so it is excluded from the global
  `api/v1` prefix in `main.ts`. Left prefixed, every certificate order fails
  validation with a 404 and nothing says why.

  ## What it returns

  The key authorization for a live challenge (`text/plain`), or `404`. Expiry is
  checked in the handler as well as by the collection's TTL index, because
  Mongo's TTL monitor runs about once a minute and removal is eventual.

  ## Why it reads from MongoDB

  The CA's validation request goes through the load balancer like any other, so
  it arrives at whichever app node the balancer picks — essentially never the
  node whose worker placed the order. Challenge tokens therefore live in the
  `acmeChallenges` collection rather than in process memory, which is what makes
  issuance work on more than one node. A single-server design that skips this
  fails intermittently in a way that looks exactly like flaky DNS.

  ## Why the guards are skipped

  - `@Public()` — the request arrives on a hostname that resolves to no tenant
    yet, which is precisely the case `HostTenantGuard` 404s. A domain being
    verified for the first time has to get past it.
  - `@SkipThrottle()` — `TrustedProxyThrottlerGuard` runs first and globally.
    Behind a TLS-passthrough load balancer every caller can share one bucket,
    and a throttled validation means no certificate for anyone until someone
    works out why. The skipped surface is one indexed lookup returning a short
    string, with no write and nothing enumerable.

  ## Testing it for real

  This request only exercises the 404 path — a real token exists for seconds
  during an order. To see the whole flow, point `ACME_DIRECTORY_URL` at a local
  Pebble instance and set `ACME_ENABLED=true`, then verify a domain.

  ⚠ `ACME_ENABLED` is `false` by default and must stay false until the Node edge
  owns port 80. While Caddy is the edge it answers its own ACME challenges and
  knows nothing of ours, so every order fails validation — and failed
  validations spend a Let's Encrypt limit that blocks orders which would have
  succeeded.
}
